{"id":"s1.3.8","slug":"multiplying-mixed-numbers","title":"Multiplying Mixed Numbers","content":"<p class=\"mb-4\">There are two main tricks for multiplying mixed numbers.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">1. <strong>FOILing:</strong> You can treat the mixed numbers as binomials and FOIL them. For $(A + \\frac{b}{c})(D + \\frac{e}{f})$, the result is $AD + A\\frac{e}{f} + D\\frac{b}{c} + (\\frac{b}{c})(\\frac{e}{f})$. This works best when the whole numbers are easily divisible by the denominators.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">2. <strong>Special Case:</strong> If the whole numbers are the same ($n$) and the fractional parts sum to 1, the result is simple. The whole part of the answer is $n(n+1)$, and the fractional part is the product of the two fractions. For example, $9\\frac{1}{3} \\times 9\\frac{2}{3} = 9(10) + (\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{2}{3}) = 90\\frac{2}{9}$.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">Sometimes, converting to improper fractions and simplifying is the easiest method.</p>","summary":"There are two main tricks for multiplying mixed numbers.","generation_guideline":"Generate a problem involving the multiplication of two mixed numbers, preferably fitting the special case where the whole numbers are the same and the fractions sum to 1.","questions":[{"id":1,"text":"Calculate $4\\frac{1}{4} \\times 8\\frac{1}{4}$. (mixed number)","answer":"35 1/16","kind":"exact","requiredForm":"mixed","tags":["arithmetic","fractions"]},{"id":2,"text":"Calculate $8\\frac{2}{3} \\times 8\\frac{1}{3}$. (mixed number)","answer":"72 2/9","kind":"exact","requiredForm":"mixed","tags":["arithmetic","fractions"]},{"id":3,"text":"Calculate $3\\frac{4}{5} \\times 3\\frac{1}{5}$. (mixed number)","answer":"12 4/25","kind":"exact","requiredForm":"mixed","tags":["arithmetic","fractions"]},{"id":4,"text":"Calculate $4\\frac{2}{3} \\times 6\\frac{1}{4}$. (mixed number)","answer":"29 1/6","kind":"exact","requiredForm":"mixed","tags":["arithmetic","fractions"]},{"id":5,"text":"Calculate $12\\frac{1}{4} \\times 8\\frac{1}{4}$. (mix